BTW, in addition to what the others wrote - don't bother. Score is
nearly immaterial in NetHack. What matters is whether you beat the game
or not, and if not, how far into the dungeon you get. The score you
reach while doing this depends so much on playing style that, while
_your_ scores for two different games are probably indicative of how far
you got (at least while you're a relative beginner), you could have a
very different set of scores from your friend even for games in which
you reached comparable points in the game.

To be a bit more precise: for normal games, the score depends mostly on
how many and how dangerous monsters you've killed. This, in turn,
depends on how much you go up and down along the way, whether you play a
chaotic character killing everything alive or a lawful one leaving
peaceful monsters alone, sheer randomness in what monsters you
encounter, and so forth. The second-most important factor is what level
you reached, but even that is dwarfed by the first. 
And here's the problem with that as a measure of progress - NetHack is
not about killing monsters, it's about getting to the end. You
(probably!) will kill a lot of monsters along the way, but that's not
the ultimate point. Scores for an ascension (i.e., a win) can be
anywhere between a couple of hundreds of thousands and a couple of
millions for _normal_ players, all of which could reasonably be said to
be roughly equally good at playing the game - they just have different
styles, some of which result in more monsters killed, some in fewer, but
all ending in victory. And for speed ascenders or even pacifists (yes,
it can be done!) the scores will typically be a lot lower, while
extinctionists will get many more points.

And now you may well ask yourself why NetHack has a score at all. Well,
probably partly because it always has. Certainly not to compare between
players. But there's one thing it is moderately useful for, and that's
for checking your own progress while you haven't ascended yet. As long
as you keep playing in the same style (as a beginner, probably hack-and-
slash), your own scores will remain more or less a measure of how far
into the game you got. So if your scores start going up, you're probably
doing better. Even for this, though, IMO your deepest dungeon level is a
better indicator.

